WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
Chapter one ended on a clue: add protein and see whether a spot grows bigger or

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.180
brighter. Now meet the bodies and chromatin a real nucleus holds.

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.460
Which have been tested as liquids, and what did those tests show?

4
00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:20.013
Some order needs no spot at all.

5
00:00:21.353 --> 00:00:26.543
After a cell divides, each chromosome partly unpacks but keeps a region of its own:

6
00:00:26.543 --> 00:00:28.013
a chromosome territory.

7
00:00:29.653 --> 00:00:33.813
Picture balls of yarn in a box, with loose loops poking across where two meet.

8
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:39.373
A sequencing method called Hi-C maps which stretches of chromatin touch.

9
00:00:40.713 --> 00:00:42.833
Its maps reveal two broad compartments.

10
00:00:44.213 --> 00:00:45.733
A is open and mostly active.

11
00:00:47.173 --> 00:00:51.933
B is closed and mostly quiet, often against a mesh under the nuclear envelope.

12
00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:56.913
Standard maps average over millions of cells, not one nucleus.

13
00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:04.787
Now the bodies. The nucleolus forms around repeated genes for ribosomal RNA.

14
00:01:06.287 --> 00:01:10.567
Mammalian nucleoli have three parts along an inside-out assembly line.

15
00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:15.027
Fibrillar centres hold the genes and copying machinery.

16
00:01:16.427 --> 00:01:19.847
New RNA emerges at their borders, where processing begins.

17
00:01:21.187 --> 00:01:25.167
The outer granular component assembles ribosomal subunits.

18
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.820
Smaller bodies sit around it, each known by what it holds.

19
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.920
Speckles hold splicing factors; Cajal bodies, the marker protein coilin; PML bodies,

20
00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.640
the PML protein. Paraspeckles are built on a long RNA called NEAT1.

21
00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.270
These bodies are real.

22
00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:50.950
They exchange molecules with the nucleoplasm, and several help with a job.

23
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.510
But how does each one form?

24
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:58.390
Only a few have had their material properties tested closely.

25
00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:04.310
The strongest liquid-phase evidence here comes from the nucleolus in the egg-forming

26
00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:10.850
cells of a frog. Its dense fibrillar and granular components behave like two liquids

27
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.550
that coexist without mixing.

28
00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:18.350
What should purified nucleolar proteins do when mixed with RNA in a tube?

29
00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:28.413
They reproduce the layering: drops with one phase inside the other.

30
00:02:29.593 --> 00:02:34.793
This is strong evidence that the frog nucleolus contains coexisting liquid phases.

31
00:02:35.917 --> 00:02:41.537
Heterochromatin, densely packed and mostly silent chromatin, adds a third mechanism.

32
00:02:42.737 --> 00:02:47.657
A protein can bridge distant stretches until the fibre collapses into a compact

33
00:02:47.657 --> 00:02:52.577
ball. Physicists count that as a phase transition of the chromatin.

34
00:02:53.757 --> 00:02:57.297
But it makes a ball of chromatin, not a liquid drop of protein.

35
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.160
The verdict splits by system.

36
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.100
In early fruit fly embryos, foci of HP1a looked liquid-like.

37
00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.280
In mouse fibroblasts, half-body FRAP found no preferential mixing inside

38
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.820
heterochromatin clusters, favouring collapsed chromatin.

39
00:03:16.933 --> 00:03:19.573
Neither study speaks for all heterochromatin.

40
00:03:20.853 --> 00:03:25.973
Whether phase separation builds a given body in a given living cell is still

41
00:03:25.973 --> 00:03:30.733
contested. The text below shows where the test itself grows weaker.
